load @term/seed/code/structure/manifold
  find manifold

load @term/seed/code/structure/set
  find set

# The models of hyperbolic space, the ways to draw the undrawable inside flat space. No model is faithful (a perfect
# flat picture is impossible, since the hyperbolic plane has no flat embedding), so each trades away something: angles,
# or straightness, or finiteness. The four standard models below each show the SAME space; choosing one is choosing
# which distortion to accept. Margenstern: "We are in the situation of a plane flying with instruments only", and the
# model is the instrument.

# The Poincare disk model: the whole hyperbolic plane drawn inside an open disk, the boundary circle the points at
# infinity. It is CONFORMAL (angles are true), so it is the model for seeing shapes, but lengths stretch without bound
# toward the rim, so equal hyperbolic tiles look ever smaller near the edge. Geodesics are circular arcs meeting the
# boundary at right angles (and diameters). This is the model the tilings are usually pictured in.
form poincare-disk
  link space, like manifold
  link boundary, like set

# The Poincare upper half-space model: the points with positive last coordinate, the bounding plane the points at
# infinity (plus one point at the top). Also CONFORMAL. Geodesics are vertical lines and semicircles centered on the
# boundary. Its gift is a distinguished point at infinity, which makes horocycles into horizontal planes, so the
# substrate's flat-lattice picture lives most naturally here, on a horosphere parallel to the boundary.
form upper-half-space
  link space, like manifold
  link boundary, like set

# The Klein (Beltrami-Klein) model: again the open disk, but now geodesics are drawn as STRAIGHT CHORDS. It trades
# conformality for straightness: lines look straight, but angles are distorted. It is the model for reasoning about
# incidence and collinearity, where only straightness matters.
form klein-model
  link space, like manifold
  link boundary, like set

# The hyperboloid (Minkowski / Lorentz) model: the hyperbolic plane realized as one sheet of a two-sheeted hyperboloid
# in one-higher Minkowski space, the set of timelike unit vectors. It is the MOST SYMMETRIC model, where the isometries
# are exactly the Lorentz transformations fixing the sheet, so it ties hyperbolic geometry directly to special
# relativity. The other three models are projections of this one. The form carries the ambient Minkowski space.
form hyperboloid-model
  link ambient, like manifold

# The four models as one object: each depicts the same hyperbolic space, related by explicit isometries (stereographic
# projections carry the hyperboloid to the disks, an inversion carries the disk to the half-space). A theorem proved in
# one transfers to all.
form hyperbolic-models
  link disk, like poincare-disk
  link half-space, like upper-half-space
  link klein, like klein-model
  link hyperboloid, like hyperboloid-model
